  "experiment": "PM1 server architecture and same-module native control",
  "fixture_sha256": "6957e4f9537d519e2133f0168fd25837a084a10e54d96166c72fd3dcf3bb8939",
  "production_threading_changed": false,
  "conclusion": "No large presentation/network overhead was established in this stationary one-client IO workload. Most measured time is inside the simulation section. Same-process server/native ratios are noisy and order-dependent; they do not establish the gain of a second simulation thread.",
  "methodology": {
    "mode": "LLVM relaxed, command blocks enabled, 12,070 game ticks, Start once, zero preparation, production 16-tick batching, IO publication configured at 10 Hz and effectively capped at 5 Hz, one compressed TCP client, main thread pinned to logical CPU 24.",
    "control": "Same process, Compiler seed, prepared Program and native module. Fresh executors use the exact production tick_batch path. Control has no Plot update, presentation or packet delivery during timing. Both paths verify RAM/lamps/screen/halt and the full ordered command trace.",
    "order": "Kernel first for odd samples; server first for even samples. Sample 1 is an excluded warmup pair; every row is retained.",
    "caveat": "Simulation-section timers include native dispatch, batch accounting and effect export; they are not a pure C profiler. Wall time includes OS scheduling. No simultaneous threaded prototype was tested."
  },
